import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="How to calculate forex position size (with examples) | Lotline" description="Learn the position size formula, see worked EUR/USD and USD/JPY examples, and understand how losing streaks hurt your account.">
  <header class="wrap" style="padding-top:1.25rem;padding-bottom:1.25rem"><a href="/" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none">Lotline</a></header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap" style="max-width:44rem;padding-bottom:3rem">
    <h1 style="max-width:none">How to calculate forex position size</h1>
    <p>Position sizing decides how much you lose when a trade fails. Fix the amount you are willing to lose first, then let the stop loss set the size.</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">The formula</h2>
    <p>Risk amount = balance x risk %. Lot size = risk amount / (stop loss in pips x pip value per lot), with pip value measured in your account currency.</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Example 1: EUR/USD, USD account</h2>
    <p>Balance 10,000, risk 1%, stop 20 pips. Risk amount is 100. One pip on a standard lot of EUR/USD is worth 10 USD. Lot size = 100 / (20 x 10) = 0.50 lots.</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Example 2: USD/JPY, USD account</h2>
    <p>A pip on JPY pairs is 0.01, so one pip on a standard lot is 1,000 JPY. At 150 JPY per USD that is about 6.67 USD. Balance 10,000, risk 1%, stop 50 pips: 100 / (50 x 6.67) = 0.30 lots.</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Why small risk matters</h2>
    <p>Losses compound against you. At 1% risk, ten losses in a row cost about 9.6%, and you need about 10.6% to recover. At 10% risk, ten losses cost about 65%, and you need about 187% to get back to even.</p>
    <p><a class="btn" href="/">Open the calculator</a></p>
    <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem">Educational content, not financial advice. Trading leveraged products carries a high risk of loss.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
